%!PS
% gs -dNOSAFER

% https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Transgender_flag
% 1999

(cowsaypride.ps) run

5 0 TRANSBLUE stripe
5 1 TRANSPINK stripe
5 2 WHITE stripe
5 3 TRANSPINK stripe
5 4 TRANSBLUE stripe

% rearrange color sequence within cow head
COWFONT findfont COWFONTSIZE scalefont setfont
% cowrender
0 0 cowpath pathbbox
pstack
exch 3 1 roll
exch sub
% dup = (cowpathheight\n) print
/cowpathheight exch def
exch sub
% dup = (cowpathwidth\n) print
/cowpathwidth exch def
PAGEWIDTH cowpathwidth sub 2 div XFUDGE mul
% dup = (clipx\n) print
PAGEHEIGHT cowpathheight sub 2 div YFUDGE mul
% dup = (clipy\n) print
cowpath clip

5 0 WHITE stripe % pink and blue split well...
5 1 WHITE stripe % ...in this part of the image.
5 2 ACEGREY stripe  % black doesn't work, and equal proportions of pink and blue are difficult
5 3 WHITE stripe % pink and blue get lost...
5 4 WHITE stripe % ...in this part of the image.

% "The pattern is such that no matter which way you fly it, it is always correct, signifying us finding correctness in our lives." [53] Monica Helms
% "LOOK: Historic Transgender Flag Flies Over The Castro". HuffPost. November 20, 2012. Archived from the original on October 23, 2022. Retrieved December 7, 2022.

% The cow head is horizontally but not vertically symmetric, and I
% would like to preserve the sentiment of correctness in any geometric
% orientation, but I think that placing more than one cow head upon
% the flag (perhaps two, or four?) would cheapen it.  Then again, this
% is a project for amusement and education as much as for any other
% purpose, and I'm fine with yet another thorn in the transphobes'
% side.

showpage
